<dialog id="sources-dialog"><div class="dialog-header"><div><div class="eyebrow">RESEARCH NOTES</div><h2>Sources &amp; method</h2></div><button id="close-sources" class="icon-button" aria-label="Close sources">×</button></div><div class="dialog-body"><div id="method-content"></div><h3>Gaia acknowledgement</h3><p>This work has made use of data from the European Space Agency (ESA) mission Gaia (<a href="https://www.cosmos.esa.int/gaia" target="_blank" rel="noopener">https://www.cosmos.esa.int/gaia</a>), processed by the Gaia Data Processing and Analysis Consortium (DPAC, <a href="https://www.cosmos.esa.int/web/gaia/dpac/consortium" target="_blank" rel="noopener">https://www.cosmos.esa.int/web/gaia/dpac/consortium</a>). Funding for the DPAC has been provided by national institutions, in particular the institutions participating in the Gaia Multilateral Agreement.</p><ul><li>Gaia Collaboration et al. (2016), <a href="https://gea.esac.esa.int/archive/documentation/GDR2/bib.html#bib173" target="_blank" rel="noopener">description of the Gaia mission</a>.</li><li>Gaia Collaboration et al. (2018b), <a href="https://gea.esac.esa.int/archive/documentation/GDR2/bib.html#bib15" target="_blank" rel="noopener">summary of Gaia DR2 contents and survey properties</a>.</li><li>ESA, 1997, <em>The Hipparcos and Tycho Catalogues</em>, ESA SP-1200. <a href="https://cdsarc.cds.unistra.fr/viz-bin/cat/I/239" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Catalogue I/239</a>.</li><li>Hoffleit et al., 1991, <em>Bright Star Catalogue, 5th Revised Edition</em>. <a href="https://cdsarc.cds.unistra.fr/viz-bin/cat/V/50" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Catalogue V/50</a>.</li></ul><h3>Historical and identification sources</h3><ul id="historical-sources"></ul><h3>Observer reconstruction</h3><p>The horizon and meridian are calculated on the celestial sphere and projected with the stars. Selecting a pole star sets its best-fit integer year within 3000 BCE–3000 CE and treats that star as the exact rotation axis. The fit uses closest approach to the mean celestial pole; the remaining angular offset is displayed. The stars retain their catalogue proper motion and precession/nutation for that epoch. Horizon, culmination, and geocentric solar altitude use the assumed axis. The minimum axis tilt retains the ephemeris sidereal rotation phase; this is an explicit idealized model, not a claim of exact historical alignment. Solar altitude is geometric; terrain, atmospheric refraction, extinction, and actual twilight visibility are not modelled. The nominal calendar uses twelve equal 30-day months, with Babylonian and Jewish names. Elapsed days are scaled by exactly 360/365; Nisannu / Nisan 1 starts when the Sun crosses the selected axis’s equator northward. This is a one-year schematic readout. Ancient clock values are approximate model outputs. The timing-star method tests <a href="https://cdli.earth/artifacts/479385" target="_blank" rel="noopener">MUL.APIN I iv 10–21</a>; morning/evening comparison follows a <a href="https://cdli.earth/articles/cdlj/2023-1" target="_blank" rel="noopener">proposed reading of Astrolabe B</a>.</p><h3>Ground sky</h3><p>The Ground sky page is a perspective projection from the observer’s position, using the same epoch, assumed pole, date, and morning/evening or culmination solution as the wheel. Looking around changes only the camera direction. The complete celestial sphere remains available, including dimmed below-horizon stars. The horizon is geometric altitude zero; the display preserves catalogue stars even in daylight and does not simulate atmospheric visibility. Constellation figures are a separate, selectable tradition layer. Connections follow their source star identifiers and move with the same epoch and observer frame. These are line figures and asterisms, not IAU area boundaries. Brightness filtering applies to stars; figure lines remain available for comparison.</p><h3>Software</h3><p>Original application code: <a href="LICENSE" download>PolyForm Noncommercial License 1.0.0</a>. <a href="THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.txt" download>Third-party notices and licenses</a>.</p><p>Solar events and coordinate rotation: <a href="https://github.com/cosinekitty/astronomy" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Astronomy Engine</a> (MIT license), vendored for offline use.</p><p>Built with <a href="https://d3js.org" target="_blank" rel="noopener">D3.js 7.9.0</a> (ISC license).</p></div></dialog>
